    overview:
      "Welcome to economic and fiscal policy. This field studies the specific machinery of government money: how a budget gets drafted and passed, how a deficit is financed and at what cost, how a tax system actually distributes its burden, and how all of that interacts with a central bank that is deliberately kept at arm's length from elected officials. Over the next forty minutes you will work through ten ideas in roughly the order a real fiscal fight actually encounters them. We start with the federal budget process itself and the debt ceiling fight that sits awkwardly beside it, then turn to the live debate over how big a dollar of stimulus or austerity actually is once it moves through the economy, and how the system's own automatic stabilizers already do a share of that work before any new law is even drafted. We then turn to the Federal Reserve's independence, the Congressional Budget Office's scoring function, and what happens when a rating agency decides your creditworthiness has changed. We close with where the money actually comes from and goes: tax expenditures buried inside the code, the entitlement trust funds whose projected depletion dates drive an entire generation of reform debates, and the fifty states whose own balanced-budget rules push the federal government into a countercyclical role almost by default. Keep one question running underneath all of it: who actually bears the cost of this choice, and when.",
