        explanation:
          "Start with the formal sequence, because very little else in this lecture makes sense without it. In principle, Congress passes a concurrent budget resolution setting overall spending and revenue targets, then twelve separate appropriations bills fund the government's discretionary programs for the coming fiscal year, which begins October 1. In practice, this sequence has broken down so often that omnibus spending packages and continuing resolutions, which simply extend prior-year funding levels rather than enacting new ones, have become the ordinary way government actually gets funded, with a genuine shutdown risk whenever neither can be agreed before a funding deadline. Sitting beside this ordinary process is reconciliation, a special procedure created by the 1974 Budget Act that allows a budget resolution's revenue, spending, and debt-limit instructions to pass the Senate with a simple majority rather than the 60 votes an ordinary bill needs to overcome a filibuster, provided every provision in the resulting bill meets the Byrd rule's requirement that it have more than an incidental effect on the budget. This is why reconciliation has become the vehicle of choice for major, party-line tax and entitlement legislation — the 2017 tax cuts and the 2021 American Rescue Plan both moved through it — precisely because it is the one path around the Senate's ordinary supermajority requirement for genuinely consequential fiscal legislation.",
