    connections:
      "Ten ideas, one recurring question: who actually bears the cost of this fiscal choice, and when does that cost land. The budget process and the debt ceiling set the procedural ground every fiscal fight is actually fought on, one determining what can pass and on what vote margin, the other a uniquely American ritual that can turn routine financing into a genuine crisis. The fiscal multiplier debate and automatic stabilizers together describe how big a dollar of government action actually is and how much of the necessary response is already happening automatically before Congress acts at all. Federal Reserve independence sits beside all of this as the deliberately separated lever that fiscal policy is not supposed to touch, while CBO scoring is the institution whose numbers determine whether a given bill can even use the reconciliation path the first concept described. A credit rating action and the entitlement trust funds are both dated, external verdicts on whether the fiscal trajectory implied by everything else in this lecture is actually sustainable, one delivered by a private agency almost immediately, the other written into law decades in advance with an automatic consequence attached. Tax expenditures and state balanced-budget rules close the loop by showing where revenue quietly leaves the system before anyone votes on it, and where a different level of government is structurally forced into exactly the opposite of the countercyclical behavior this entire lecture has been describing. As you follow any live US fiscal debate from here forward, ask which of these ten mechanisms is actually doing the work, and which political actor is counting on the cost landing on someone else, or on a future Congress, before anyone has to answer for it today.",
