          "Now turn to the market verdict on everything covered so far. France's public debt has risen to roughly 110% of GDP, among the highest in the eurozone's core economies, well above the 60% reference threshold covered in this course's EU-fiscal-rules concept, and the three major credit rating agencies have downgraded France's sovereign rating on multiple occasions over the past decade, most recently in cycles tied directly to the repeated 49.3 budget crises and parliamentary fragmentation covered earlier in this lecture, with agency commentary explicitly citing political instability and the difficulty of passing credible deficit-reduction measures as central to their decisions, not merely the debt level itself [8]. The mechanism linking the political dysfunction concepts earlier in this lecture to the rating actions here is direct: a government that must repeatedly resort to 49.3 to pass a budget, and whose Haut Conseil has repeatedly flagged its own forecasts as overly optimistic, sends exactly the kind of governance-risk signal that rating-agency methodology, covered in this course's US lecture for a different context, weighs alongside the headline deficit and debt figures themselves.",
