        explanation:
          "We close with the fight that shows every earlier mechanism in this lecture actually operating together on one real, contested policy. France's pension system, like Germany's, operates substantially on a pay-as-you-go basis managed through the PLFSS track covered earlier in this lecture, and successive governments across more than three decades have repeatedly attempted to raise the legal retirement age or otherwise tighten the system's financing, facing sustained public protest and repeated legislative difficulty each time [9]. The 2023 reform, raising the minimum retirement age from 62 to 64, was legislated through the PLFSS specifically, forced through using 49.3 on the social-security financing text after the government concluded it lacked an ordinary floor majority, survived a resulting motion de censure by a narrow margin, and was challenged before but ultimately upheld by the Conseil constitutionnel, a sequence that traces directly through the loi de financement structure, the 49.3 mechanism, and the credibility stakes covered across this entire lecture.",
