    connections:
      "Ten ideas, one recurring test: can the government actually get this through, and at what institutional cost if it has to force the issue. The loi de finances process and Article 40's narrow amendment power set the baseline reality that French budget politics is fought mostly at the drafting stage, not the floor vote, while Article 49.3 is the tool a government without a reliable majority reaches for once that baseline process looks uncertain. The Haut Conseil des finances publiques and the Cour des comptes are the two independent checks on whether the resulting numbers and their execution can actually be trusted, one scrutinizing the forecast before a bill is even debated, the other auditing what actually happened afterward with real judicial teeth attached. The PLFSS's separate track and France's structurally high prélèvements obligatoires together explain why so much of the fiscal action in this lecture, pension reform chief among it, runs through a parallel budget most casual coverage overlooks entirely. The Banque de France's institutional role inside the Eurosystem mirrors the monetary-fiscal separation this course describes elsewhere, while the credit-rating and debt-trajectory story is the market's own verdict on whether the political fragmentation running through every earlier concept is actually being managed. The pension-reform fight closes the loop, showing all nine earlier mechanisms operating together on one real, bitterly contested policy. As you follow any live French fiscal fight from here forward, ask which track it is actually on, whether the government can avoid reaching for 49.3, and what the Haut Conseil, the Cour des comptes, and the rating agencies are each independently saying about whether the numbers behind it can be trusted.",
